# Type scale

Take every text size from this scale. Do not make up a new size for one card.

Each role has one size on narrow screens, such as a phone, and one on wide screens. The sizes change at 768px. The Civica theme multiplies every card size by Home Assistant's font size scale, `--ha-font-size-scale`, so all text grows together if the scale is raised. Browser and app zoom make it larger too.

## Sizes for each role

Narrow screens, below 768px:

| Role | Size | Line height | Weight |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Heading level 1 | 30px | 40px | Bold, 700 |
| Heading level 2 | 22px | 32px | Bold, 700 |
| Heading level 3 | 19px | 24px | Bold, 700 |
| Heading level 4 | 16px | 24px | Bold, 700 |
| Heading level 5 | 14px | 24px | Bold, 700 |
| Lead paragraph | 19px | 32px | Light, 300 |
| Body text | 16px | 24px | Regular, 400 |
| Small text | 14px | 24px | Regular, 400 |

Wide screens, from 768px:

| Role | Size | Line height | Weight |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Heading level 1 | 44px | 56px | Bold, 700 |
| Heading level 2 | 30px | 40px | Bold, 700 |
| Heading level 3 | 22px | 32px | Bold, 700 |
| Heading level 4 | 19px | 32px | Bold, 700 |
| Heading level 5 | 16px | 24px | Bold, 700 |
| Lead paragraph | 24px | 40px | Light, 300 |
| Body text | 19px | 32px | Regular, 400 |
| Small text | 16px | 24px | Regular, 400 |

Documentation pages and custom panels follow both tables. A Home Assistant theme cannot change a size with the width of the screen, so cards on a dashboard take their sizes from the wide-screen table:

| Role in a card | Size | Civica or Home Assistant variable |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Card heading | 22px | `civica-font-size-heading` |
| Main reading | Sized to fit the value and unit, about 32px to 48px | None. Size it to fit the card |
| Body text and main controls | 19px | `civica-font-size-body` |
| Supporting text | 16px | `civica-font-size-small` |

Card line heights follow the same table: 32px for body text, 24px for supporting text, and 32px for a card heading.

## Spacing

Keep the space around text on this scale too:

| Element | Space above | Space below |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Paragraph | None | 16px |
| Heading level 2 | 32px on narrow screens, 40px on wide screens | 16px |
| Heading levels 3, 4 and 5 | 24px | 16px |
| Lead paragraph | None | 32px on narrow screens, 40px on wide screens |
| Bulleted or numbered list | None | 16px, with 8px between items |

## Specimen

This specimen sets each role at its wide-screen size, with text from an invented household's dashboard. On a phone, every role uses its narrow size instead.

<%=
  samples = [
    ['Hughes home', 'Heading level 1', 44, 700, 56],
    ['Downstairs', 'Heading level 2', 30, 700, 40],
    ['Living room', 'Heading level 3 and card heading', 22, 700, 32],
    ['Heating schedule', 'Heading level 4', 19, 700, 32],
    ['Rooms in this home', 'Heading level 5', 16, 700, 24],
    ['Every room has its own schedule.', 'Lead paragraph', 24, 300, 40],
    ['The heating is on until 22:00.', 'Body text and main controls', 19, 400, 32],
    ['Updated 14:05', 'Small text and supporting text', 16, 400, 24]
  ]
  items = samples.map do |text, role, size, weight, line|
    name = { 300 => 'light', 400 => 'regular', 700 => 'bold' }[weight]
    %(<div style="margin:0 0 16px"><p style="font-family:Roboto,Helvetica Neue,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:#{size}px;font-weight:#{weight};line-height:#{line}px;margin:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere">#{text}</p><p style="font-size:16px;line-height:24px;margin:0">#{role}, #{size}px #{name}, line height #{line}px</p></div>)
  end
  %(<figure style="margin:0 0 32px">#{items.join}<figcaption>Each role at its wide-screen size. The text is a sample, so it is not marked up as a heading.</figcaption></figure>)
%>

## Narrow and wide screens compared

This comparison shows how much each size changes between narrow and wide screens. Headings change most. Body and small text change by 3px or less, so they stay easy to read on a phone.

<%=
  hex = ->(id) { govuk_colour(id).fetch('hex') }
  ink, grid, wide_c, narrow_c = hex.('black'), hex.('black-tint-80'), hex.('blue'), hex.('magenta')
  roles = [
    ['Heading level 1', 44, 30],
    ['Heading level 2', 30, 22],
    ['Heading level 3', 22, 19],
    ['Heading level 4', 19, 16],
    ['Heading level 5', 16, 14],
    ['Lead paragraph', 24, 19],
    ['Body text', 19, 16],
    ['Small text', 16, 14]
  ]
  x_of = ->(px) { 60 + px * 4 }
  svg = []
  [0, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50].each { |v| svg << %(<line x1="#{x_of.(v)}" y1="8" x2="#{x_of.(v)}" y2="#{roles.size * 44 - 4}" stroke="#{grid}" stroke-width="1"/><text x="#{x_of.(v)}" y="#{roles.size * 44 + 8}" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#{ink}">#{v}</text>) }
  svg << %(<text x="#{x_of.(25)}" y="#{roles.size * 44 + 21}" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#{ink}">Size (px)</text>)
  roles.each_with_index do |(role, wide, narrow), i|
    y = 18 + i * 44
    svg << %(<text x="0" y="#{y}" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#{ink}">#{role}</text>)
    svg << %(<text x="0" y="#{y + 14}" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">Wide</text><rect x="60" y="#{y + 4}" width="#{wide * 4}" height="12" fill="#{wide_c}"/><text x="#{x_of.(wide) + 5}" y="#{y + 14}" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">#{wide}px</text>)
    svg << %(<text x="0" y="#{y + 29}" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">Narrow</text><rect x="60" y="#{y + 19}" width="#{narrow * 4}" height="12" fill="#{narrow_c}"/><text x="#{x_of.(narrow) + 5}" y="#{y + 29}" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">#{narrow}px</text>)
  end
  rows = roles.map { |role, wide, narrow| %(<tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="row" class="govuk-table__header">#{role}</th><td class="govuk-table__cell">#{wide}px</td><td class="govuk-table__cell">#{narrow}px</td></tr>) }
  height = roles.size * 44 + 28
  %(<div class="c-colour-example"><figure style="margin:0 0 20px">) +
    %(<svg role="img" aria-labelledby="scale-compared-title scale-compared-desc" viewBox="0 0 320 #{height}" style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:480px;height:auto;font-family:Roboto,Helvetica Neue,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"><title id="scale-compared-title">Bar chart comparing each type size on wide and narrow screens</title><desc id="scale-compared-desc">Each role has a wide-screen bar and a narrow-screen bar, with the size in pixels at the end of each bar. Heading level 1 drops from 44px to 30px, level 2 from 30px to 22px, level 3 from 22px to 19px, level 4 from 19px to 16px and level 5 from 16px to 14px. The lead paragraph drops from 24px to 19px, body text from 19px to 16px and small text from 16px to 14px. The table after the chart gives every size.</desc><g aria-hidden="true">#{svg.join}</g></svg>) +
    %(<figcaption>Type sizes in px on wide and narrow screens, before the font size scale.</figcaption></figure>) +
    %(<details class="govuk-details"><summary class="govuk-details__summary"><span class="govuk-details__summary-text">Show the data: type sizes on wide and narrow screens</span></summary><div class="govuk-details__text"><div class="civica-table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Type sizes on wide and narrow screens"><table class="govuk-table"><caption class="govuk-table__caption govuk-table__caption--s">Type sizes on wide and narrow screens</caption><thead class="govuk-table__head"><tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Role</th><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Wide screens</th><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Narrow screens</th></tr></thead><tbody class="govuk-table__body">#{rows.join}</tbody></table></div></div></details></div>)
%>

<h2 id="native-home-assistant-sizes">Native Home Assistant sizes</h2>

Native Home Assistant screens are denser than a Civica card, so they keep smaller sizes. The Civica theme raises each native size by one step, to these values:

| Home Assistant variable | Size | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ha-font-size-l` | 19px | Larger native text |
| `ha-font-size-m` | 16px | Native controls and body text |
| `ha-font-size-s` | 14px | Supporting labels |
| `ha-font-size-xs` | 12px | Compact extra text, such as a secondary detail in a row |

[Web-app typography](typography-web-app.html#bg-typ-04) explains why native screens stay dense.

## Set sizes in a card

Use the variable, not a pixel value, so the size follows the font size scale. Each variable is written as a `calc()` that multiplies the base size by `--ha-font-size-scale`.

```css
.reading-label {
  font-size: var(--civica-font-size-small);
  line-height: 1.5;
}
```


Heading cards already use the card heading size. [Headings](typography-headings.html#heading-cards) explains how.
